Mr. Brennan has 7 boys and 4 girls in his statistics class. He wants to pick 3 boys and 2 girls for a group presentation. Since the order does not matter, we need to use combinations.

First, we calculate the number of ways to choose 3 boys out of 7:
\[
\binom{7}{3} = \frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} = \frac{7!}{3! \cdot 4!} = \frac{5040}{6 \cdot 24} = 35
