To express the quotient \(413_5 \div 2_5\) in base 5, we can convert the numbers to base 10, perform the division, and then convert the result back to base 5.

First, convert \(413_5\) to base 10:
\[
413_5 = 4 \times 5^2 + 1 \times 5^1 + 3 \times 5^0 = 4 \times 25 + 1 \times 5 + 3 \times 1 = 100 + 5 + 3 = 108_{10}
\]

Next, convert \(2_5\) to base 10:
\[
2_5 = 2_{10}
\]

Perform the division in base 10:
\[
108_{10} \div 2_{10} = 54_{10}
\]

Convert the result \(54_{10}\) back to base 5:
\[
54 \div 5 = 10 \text{ remainder } 4 \quad \text{(rightmost digit)}
\]
\[
10 \div 5 = 2 \text{ remainder } 0 \quad \text{(next digit)}
\]
\[
2 \div 5 = 0 \text{ remainder } 2 \quad \text{(leftmost digit)}
\]

Reading the remainders from bottom to top, we get \(204_5\).

Thus, the quotient of \(413_5 \div 2_5\) expressed in base 5 is \(\boxed{204_5}\).
