To find the area of triangle \(OAB\), we use the cross product of vectors \(\overrightarrow{OA}\) and \(\overrightarrow{OB}\). The formula for the area is \(\frac{1}{2}\) times the magnitude of the cross product.

Given vectors:
\[
\overrightarrow{OA} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \overrightarrow{OB} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]

First, compute the cross product \(\overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{OB}\):
\[
\overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{OB} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
1 & 2 & 3 \\
-3 & -2 & 1
\end{vmatrix}
= \mathbf{i}(2 \cdot 1 - 3 \cdot (-2)) - \mathbf{j}(1 \cdot 1 - 3 \cdot (-3)) + \mathbf{k}(1 \cdot (-2) - 2 \cdot (-3))
\]
\[
= \mathbf{i}(2 + 6) - \mathbf{j}(1 + 9) + \mathbf{k}(-2 + 6)
\]
\[
= 8\mathbf{i} - 10\mathbf{j} + 4\mathbf{k}
\]

The magnitude of the cross product vector is:
\[
| \overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{OB} | = \sqrt{8^2 + (-10)^2 + 4^2} = \sqrt{64 + 100 + 16} = \sqrt{180} = 6\sqrt{5}
\]

The area of triangle \(OAB\) is half of this magnitude:
\[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times 6\sqrt{5} = 3\sqrt{5}
\]

Thus, the area of triangle \(OAB\) is \(\boxed{3\sqrt{5}}\). 11%|█         | 44/400 [1:11:34<5:45:39, 58.26s/it] 11%|█▏        | 45/400 [1:12:02<4:51:50, 49.33s/it]
