To compute the infinite series

\[
\sum_{n = 1}^\infty \frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}},
\]

where \(F_n\) denotes the \(n\)th Fibonacci number, we start by expressing the general term using the Fibonacci recurrence relation. We use the identity:

\[
\frac{F_{n + 1}}{F_n F_{n + 2}} = \frac{1}{F_n} - \frac{1}{F_{n + 2}}.
\]

This allows us to rewrite the series as a telescoping series:

\[
\sum_{n = 1}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{F_n} - \frac{1}{F_{n + 2}} \right).
\]

Expanding the first few terms of this series, we see that it telescopes:

\[
\left( \frac{1}{F_1} - \frac{1}{F_3} \right) + \left( \frac{1}{F_2} - \frac{1}{F_4} \right) + \left( \frac{1}{F_3} - \frac{1}{F_5} \right) + \left( \frac{1}{F_4} - \frac{1}{F_6} \right) + \cdots.
\]

When we sum these terms, most intermediate terms cancel out, leaving:

\[
\frac{1}{F_1} + \frac{1}{F_2} - \lim_{N \to \infty} \left( \frac{1}{F_{N+1}} + \frac{1}{F_{N+2}} \right).
\]

Since \(F_n\) grows exponentially, \(\frac{1}{F_{N+1}}\) and \(\frac{1}{F_{N+2}}\) approach zero as \(N\) approaches infinity. Therefore, the sum converges to:

\[
\frac{1}{F_1} + \frac{1}{F_2} = 1 + 1 = 2.
\]

Thus, the sum of the series is:

\[
\boxed{2}
\] 28%|██▊       | 114/400 [2:51:47<7:50:15, 98.66s/it]
