Let me think. So, since each car independently chooses a lane, the total number of possible ways the cars can stack up is 3^6, since each car has three choices. But we need to subtract the cases where one or more lanes are empty. 

Ah, right, so this is a classic inclusion-exclusion problem. The number of onto functions from a set of six elements to a set of three elements. So, the formula for the number of onto functions is 3! * S(6,3), where S(6,3) is the Stirling numbers of the second kind. But I might not remember that formula exactly, but maybe I can compute it using inclusion-exclusion.

