To find the measure of angle \( A \) in the regular pentagon \( FGHIJ \) when the sides are extended to form a star, we start by noting that a regular pentagon has each interior angle equal to \( 108^\circ \). 

When the sides of the pentagon are extended, they form a star known as a pentagram. In a regular pentagram, each tip is an isosceles triangle with a vertex angle of \( 36^\circ \). This is because the pentagram can be thought of as a collection of triangles where each tip is formed by extending the sides of the pentagon, creating a star with five points.

The measure of the angle at each tip of the pentagram is calculated using the properties of a regular pentagram. The formula for the internal angle of a regular star polygon {n/m} is:

\[
\text{Internal angle} = \frac{180^\circ (n - 2m)}{n}
\]

For a pentagram, which is {5/2}, we have \( n = 5 \) and \( m = 2 \). Plugging these values into the formula gives:

\[
\text{Internal angle} = \frac{180^\circ (5 - 4)}{5} = \frac{180^\circ \times 1}{5} = 36^\circ
\]

Thus, the measure of angle \( A \) is \( 36^\circ \).

\[
\boxed{36}
\] 46%|████▌     | 182/400 [4:33:36<4:07:34, 68.14s/it] 46%|████▌     | 183/400 [4:34:00<3:19:02, 55.03s/it] 46%|████▌     | 184/400 [4:34:20<2:40:24, 44.56s/it]
