Charlie is riding his unicycle, and we need to find his speed in inches per second. The unicycle's wheel has a radius of 9 inches and makes 2 revolutions every 3 seconds.

First, we calculate the angular speed. The wheel makes 2 revolutions every 3 seconds, which is \( \frac{2}{3} \) revolutions per second. Converting this to radians per second, we use the fact that one revolution is \( 2\pi \) radians:

\[
\text{Angular speed} = \left( \frac{2}{3} \text{ revolutions per second} \right) \times (2\pi \text{ radians per revolution}) = \frac{4\pi}{3} \text{ radians per second}
\]

Next, we use the formula for linear speed, which is the product of angular speed and radius:

\[
\text{Linear speed} = \text{Angular speed} \times \text{Radius} = \left( \frac{4\pi}{3} \text{ radians per second} \right) \times 9 \text{ inches}
\]

Calculating this, we get:

\[
\text{Linear speed} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \times 9 = 12\pi \text{ inches per second}
\]

Thus, the unicycle's speed is \(\boxed{12\pi}\) inches per second.
