The function \( f(x) = \left\lfloor \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^x \right\rfloor \) is defined for all \( x \) in the interval \([0, \infty)\) such that \( f(x) \) is a real number. 

First, we note that \( \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^x \) is real only when \( x \) is an integer because for non-integer \( x \), the expression would involve complex numbers. Therefore, we only consider integer values of \( x \).

Evaluating the function for integer values of \( x \):

- For \( x = 0 \): \( \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^0 = 1 \), so \( f(0) = \left\lfloor 1 \right\rfloor = 1 \).
- For \( x = 1 \): \( \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^1 = -\frac{5}{8} \), so \( f(1) = \left\lfloor -\frac{5}{8} \right\rfloor = -1 \).
- For \( x = 2 \): \( \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^2 = \frac{25}{64} \), so \( f(2) = \left\lfloor \frac{25}{64} \right\rfloor = 0 \).
- For \( x = 3 \): \( \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^3 = -\frac{125}{512} \), so \( f(3) = \left\lfloor -\frac{125}{512} \right\rfloor = -1 \).
- For \( x = 4 \): \( \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^4 = \frac{625}{4096} \), so \( f(4) = \left\lfloor \frac{625}{4096} \right\rfloor = 0 \).
- For \( x = 5 \): \( \left( -\frac{5}{8} \right)^5 = -\frac{3125}{32768} \), so \( f(5) = \left\lfloor -\frac{3125}{32768} \right\rfloor = -1 \).

From these evaluations, we observe that the function \( f(x) \) alternates between 0 and -1 for integer values of \( x \geq 2 \), and it takes the value 1 at \( x = 0 \) and -1 at \( x = 1 \). Therefore, the distinct values in the range of \( f(x) \) are \(-1\), \(0\), and \(1\).

Thus, the number of distinct values in the range of \( f(x) \) is \(\boxed{3}\). 57%|█████▋    | 229/400 [5:45:10<4:18:53, 90.84s/it]  57%|█████▊    | 230/400 [5:45:30<3:17:38, 69.76s/it]
