To find the value of \( a \) that makes the function \( f(n) \) continuous, we need to ensure that the left-hand limit and the right-hand limit at \( n = a \) are equal and both equal \( f(a) \).

The function \( f(n) \) is defined as:
\[ f(n) = 
\begin{cases} 
4n + 3 & \text{if } n < a, \\
7n - 12 & \text{if } n \geq a.
\end{cases}
\]

For continuity at \( n = a \):
1. The left-hand limit as \( n \) approaches \( a \) from below is \( 4a + 3 \).
2. The right-hand limit as \( n \) approaches \( a \) from above is \( 7a - 12 \).

Setting these equal gives:
\[ 4a + 3 = 7a - 12 \]

Solving for \( a \):
\[
4a + 3 = 7a - 12 \\
3 = 3a - 12 \\
15 = 3a \\
a = 5
\]

Thus, the value of \( a \) that makes the function continuous is \(\boxed{5}\).
