To find the largest integer less than 2010 that has a remainder of 5 when divided by 7, a remainder of 10 when divided by 11, and a remainder of 10 when divided by 13, we use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.

We start with the given conditions:
1. \( N \equiv 5 \pmod{7} \)
2. \( N \equiv 10 \pmod{11} \)
3. \( N \equiv 10 \pmod{13} \)

First, we solve the system of congruences step by step.

1. Express \( N \) in terms of 7:
   \[
   N = 7a + 5
   \]

2. Substitute into the second congruence:
   \[
   7a + 5 \equiv 10 \pmod{11} \implies 7a \equiv 5 \pmod{11}
   \]
   The multiplicative inverse of 7 modulo 11 is 8 (since \( 7 \times 8 \equiv 1 \pmod{11} \)):
   \[
   a \equiv 5 \times 8 \pmod{11} \implies a \equiv 40 \pmod{11} \implies a \equiv 7 \pmod{11}
   \]
   Thus, \( a = 11b + 7 \), and substituting back:
   \[
   N = 7(11b + 7) + 5 = 77b + 54
   \]

3. Substitute into the third congruence:
   \[
   77b + 54 \equiv 10 \pmod{13}
   \]
   Simplify modulo 13:
   \[
   77 \equiv 12 \pmod{13} \quad \text{and} \quad 54 \equiv 2 \pmod{13}
   \]
   \[
   12b + 2 \equiv 10 \pmod{13} \implies 12b \equiv 8 \pmod{13}
   \]
   The multiplicative inverse of 12 modulo 13 is 12:
   \[
   b \equiv 8 \times 12 \pmod{13} \implies b \equiv 96 \pmod{13} \implies b \equiv 5 \pmod{13}
   \]
   Thus, \( b = 13c + 5 \), and substituting back:
   \[
   N = 77(13c + 5) + 54 = 1001c + 439
   \]

The solutions are of the form \( N = 1001c + 439 \). We need the largest \( N \) less than 2010:
   \[
   1001c + 439 < 2010 \implies 1001c < 1571 \implies c < 1.56
   \]
   The largest integer \( c \) is 1, giving:
   \[
   N = 1001 \times 1 + 439 = 1440
   \]

Thus, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies all the given conditions is \(\boxed{1440}\). 81%|████████  | 324/400 [8:03:22<3:09:39, 149.74s/it] 81%|████████▏ | 325/400 [8:03:48<2:20:48, 112.65s/it]
