Karla drove her vehicle 12,000 miles last year. To determine how many gallons of gasoline she would have saved if she drove a fuel-efficient hybrid car averaging 48 miles per gallon instead of her SUV, which averaged 15 miles per gallon, we follow these steps:

1. Calculate the gasoline used with the SUV:
   \[
   \text{Gasoline used with SUV} = \frac{12,000 \text{ miles}}{15 \text{ mpg}} = 800 \text{ gallons}
   \]

2. Calculate the gasoline used with the hybrid car:
   \[
   \text{Gasoline used with hybrid} = \frac{12,000 \text{ miles}}{48 \text{ mpg}} = 250 \text{ gallons}
   \]

3. Determine the savings by subtracting the gasoline used with the hybrid car from the gasoline used with the SUV:
   \[
   \text{Gasoline saved} = 800 \text{ gallons} - 250 \text{ gallons} = 550 \text{ gallons}
   \]

Thus, Karla would have saved \boxed{550} gallons of gasoline.
