To determine the primes \( p \) for which the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) has no solutions, we need to consider the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 8 and \( p \). The congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) has a solution if and only if \( \gcd(8, p) = 1 \). Since \( p \) is a prime, this condition is satisfied if and only if \( p \) does not divide 8.

The prime factorization of 8 is \( 2^3 \), so the only prime divisor is 2. Therefore, the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) has no solutions when \( p = 2 \).

Testing other primes confirms that for any prime \( p \) other than 2, the congruence has a solution. For example, modulo 3, 5, 7, and 11, the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) always has a solution because 8 is coprime to these primes.

Thus, the only prime \( p \) for which the congruence has no solution is 2. The sum of all such primes is therefore 2.

\[
\boxed{2}
\]
