To determine the minimum value of the function \( f(x) = |x - p| + |x - 15| + |x - p - 15| \) for \( x \) in the interval \( p \leq x \leq 15 \), we first simplify the function within this interval.

Given \( 0 < p < 15 \), we note that the critical points are \( x = p \), \( x = 15 \), and \( x = p + 15 \). However, since \( p + 15 \) is outside the interval \( p \leq x \leq 15 \), we only consider the behavior of the function within this interval.

Within the interval \( p \leq x \leq 15 \):
1. \( |x - p| = x - p \)
2. \( |x - 15| = 15 - x \)
3. \( |x - p - 15| = p + 15 - x \)

Substituting these into \( f(x) \):
\[
f(x) = (x - p) + (15 - x) + (p + 15 - x)
\]

Simplifying this expression:
\[
f(x) = x - p + 15 - x + p + 15 - x = -x + 30
\]

Thus, within the interval \( p \leq x \leq 15 \), the function simplifies to \( f(x) = -x + 30 \), which is a linear function with a slope of -1. This linear function is decreasing on the interval, meaning its minimum value occurs at the right endpoint \( x = 15 \).

Evaluating \( f(x) \) at \( x = 15 \):
\[
f(15) = |15 - p| + |15 - 15| + |15 - p - 15| = (15 - p) + 0 + p = 15
\]

Therefore, the minimum value of \( f(x) \) in the interval \( p \leq x \leq 15 \) is \(\boxed{15}\). 98%|█████████▊| 390/400 [9:21:26<12:09, 72.93s/it]
